-- §6.2 T4 — THE OPEN HALF of the #1155 closedness pair. Identical to
-- s6-2-t3-closed-goal-reported.mdk except for ONE TOKEN on the last line: `[1]`
-- becomes `[]`.
--
-- SPEC ANSWER (hand-derived, NOT captured): the goal is now
-- `Sh (Q Int Bool (List ?e))` with `?e` an unbound metavariable that nothing in
-- the program determines and no scheme quantifies (`main` is not generalized).
-- Both impls still MATCH it -- each carries a bare variable at argument 2
-- (`z`), and `matchStep`'s `TyVar` arm binds any mono including an open one --
-- and they are still ⊑-incomparable, so the selector reaches its
-- no-unique-minimum arm with the SAME candidate set as the sibling.
--
-- §6.2 T4: a goal still not closed at quiescence is genuinely undetermined and
-- is REJECTED as ambiguous, never committed to a default instance. A match
-- survives every binding of `?e`, so no later unification can leave fewer than
-- two incomparable matches: the goal is rejected at its own occurrence on every
-- verb (R-SC3, #1183), with the same code as its closed sibling.
--
-- Before R-SC3 this file ACCEPTED: it committed to the first-declared impl under
-- a declaration-order warning and printed `1`, or `2` with the two `impl`
-- blocks swapped. That declaration-order dependence is what the reject removes.
data Q a b c = Q a b c

interface Sh a where
  sh : a -> Int

impl Sh (Q Int y z) where
  sh _ = 1

impl Sh (Q x Bool z) where
  sh _ = 2

main = println (sh (Q 1 True []))
